{"task_id": "HumanEval/40", "solution": "def triples_sum_to_zero(l: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    triples_sum_to_zero takes a list of integers as an input.\n    it returns True if there are three distinct elements in the list that\n    sum to zero, and False otherwise.\n\n    >>> triples_sum_to_zero([1, 3, 5, 0])\n    False\n    >>> triples_sum_to_zero([1, 3, -2, 1])\n    True\n    >>> triples_sum_to_zero([1, 2, 3, 7])\n    False\n    >>> triples_sum_to_zero([2, 4, -5, 3, 9, 7])\n    True\n    >>> triples_sum_to_zero([1])\n    False\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(l)\n    if n < 3:\n        return False\n\n    # Sort the list to enable the two-pointer approach.\n    # The problem implies 'l' contains integers, so sorted() will work.\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(l)\n\n    for i in range(n - 2):\n        # Optimization: Skip duplicate elements for the first pointer (by value).\n        # This prevents redundant calculations when the current fixed number is the same\n        # as the previous one, ensuring we consider distinct combinations efficiently.\n        if i > 0 and sorted_numbers[i] == sorted_numbers[i-1]:\n            continue\n\n        left = i + 1\n        right = n - 1\n\n        while left < right:\n            current_sum = sorted_numbers[i] + sorted_numbers[left] + sorted_numbers[right]\n\n            if current_sum == 0:\n                # Found a triple that sums to zero\n                return True\n            elif current_sum < 0:\n                # Sum is too small, move 'left' pointer to increase the sum\n                left += 1\n            else: # current_sum > 0\n                # Sum is too large, move 'right' pointer to decrease the sum\n                right -= 1\n    \n    # No triple found after checking all combinations\n    return False"}
