{"task_id": "HumanEval/80", "solution": "def is_happy(s: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a string is 'happy' based on length and distinct consecutive characters.\n    A string is happy if its length is at least 3 and every 3 consecutive letters are distinct.\n    \"\"\"\n    MIN_HAPPY_LENGTH = 3\n\n    if len(s) < MIN_HAPPY_LENGTH:\n        return False\n\n    for i in range(len(s) - MIN_HAPPY_LENGTH + 1):\n        substring = s[i : i + MIN_HAPPY_LENGTH]\n        # Check if all three characters in the substring are distinct.\n        if not (substring[0] != substring[1] and substring[0] != substring[2] and substring[1] != substring[2]):\n            return False\n\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/80", "solution": "def _is_triplet_distinct(triplet):\n    \"\"\"Checks if all three characters in a 3-character string are distinct.\n    Assumes input 'triplet' is a string of length 3.\"\"\"\n    return triplet[0] != triplet[1] and \\\n           triplet[0] != triplet[2] and \\\n           triplet[1] != triplet[2]\ndef is_happy(s):\n    \"\"\"You are given a string s.\n    Your task is to check if the string is happy or not.\n    A string is happy if its length is at least 3 and every 3 consecutive letters are distinct\n    For example:\n    is_happy(a) => False\n    is_happy(aa) => False\n    is_happy(abcd) => True\n    is_happy(aabb) => False\n    is_happy(adb) => True\n    is_happy(xyy) => False\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(s) < 3:\n        return False\n\n    for i in range(len(s) - 2):\n        triplet = s[i:i+3]\n        if not _is_triplet_distinct(triplet):\n            return False\n    \n    return True"}
