{"task_id": "HumanEval/80", "solution": "def is_happy(s):\n    \"\"\"Checks if a string is happy based on length and distinct consecutive characters.\n    A string is happy if its length is at least 3 and every 3 consecutive characters must be distinct.\"\"\"\n    MIN_HAPPY_LENGTH = 3\n\n    if len(s) < MIN_HAPPY_LENGTH:\n        return False\n\n    # Iterate through the string to check all 3-character consecutive substrings.\n    # The loop goes up to len(s) - 3 to allow access to s[i+2].\n    for i in range(len(s) - (MIN_HAPPY_LENGTH - 1)):\n        # Check if s[i], s[i+1], and s[i+2] are all distinct.\n        if s[i] == s[i+1] or s[i] == s[i+2] or s[i+1] == s[i+2]:\n            return False\n\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/80", "solution": "def is_happy(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a string is happy. A string is happy if its length is at least 3 and every 3 consecutive letters are distinct.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(s, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a string.\")\n\n    MIN_HAPPY_LENGTH = 3\n    if len(s) < MIN_HAPPY_LENGTH:\n        return False\n\n    # Iterate through the string, checking every sequence of 3 consecutive characters.\n    for i in range(len(s) - (MIN_HAPPY_LENGTH - 1)):\n        char1, char2, char3 = s[i], s[i+1], s[i+2]\n        \n        # Determine if 3 consecutive characters are distinct.\n        if char1 == char2 or char1 == char3 or char2 == char3:\n            return False\n            \n    return True"}
