{"task_id": "HumanEval/100", "solution": "def make_a_pile(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer n, you have to make a pile of n levels of stones.\n    The first level has n stones.\n    The number of stones in the next level is:\n        - the next odd number if n is odd.\n        - the next even number if n is even.\n    Return the number of stones in each level in a list, where element at index\n    i represents the number of stones in the level (i+1).\n\n    Examples:\n    >>> make_a_pile(3)\n    [3, 5, 7]\n    \"\"\"\n    # Validate input 'n' as a positive integer per FR1 in the design.\n    # Using a standard ValueError as a custom exception class is not included in the bare function output.\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a positive integer.\")\n\n    pile = [n]\n    current_stones = n\n\n    # Calculate stones for the remaining n-1 levels.\n    # The rule for determining the next level's stones (next odd if n is odd,\n    # next even if n is even) simplifies to adding 2, because adding 2 to an\n    # odd number yields the next odd, and adding 2 to an even number yields\n    # the next even.\n    for _ in range(n - 1):\n        next_stones = current_stones + 2\n        pile.append(next_stones)\n        current_stones = next_stones\n        \n    return pile"}
