{"task_id": "HumanEval/105", "solution": "from typing import List, Dict\nDIGIT_NAMES_MAP: Dict[int, str] = {\n    1: \"One\",\n    2: \"Two\",\n    3: \"Three\",\n    4: \"Four\",\n    5: \"Five\",\n    6: \"Six\",\n    7: \"Seven\",\n    8: \"Eight\",\n    9: \"Nine\",\n}\ndef filter_valid_numbers(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"Filters a list of integers, retaining only those between 1 and 9 inclusive.\"\"\"\n    return [n for n in numbers if 1 <= n <= 9]\ndef map_digit_to_name(digit: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Maps a single digit (1-9) to its English word representation.\n    Assumes the input digit is valid (1-9).\"\"\"\n    return DIGIT_NAMES_MAP[digit]\ndef by_length(arr: List[int]) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"Given an array of integers, sorts valid numbers (1-9), reverses them, and maps to English words.\n    Handles empty arrays and ignores invalid numbers.\"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return []\n\n    # 1. Filter valid numbers (1-9 inclusive) as per FR1.\n    filtered_numbers = filter_valid_numbers(arr)\n\n    # 2. Sort in ascending order as per FR2.\n    # NFR1: Python's list.sort() uses Timsort, which is efficient for typical array sizes (O(N log N)).\n    filtered_numbers.sort()\n\n    # 3. Reverse the sorted array as per FR3.\n    reversed_numbers = filtered_numbers[::-1]\n\n    # 4. Replace each digit by its corresponding name as per FR4.\n    # NFR1: List comprehension is efficient for transforming elements.\n    result_names = [map_digit_to_name(n) for n in reversed_numbers]\n\n    return result_names"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/106", "solution": "def f(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates a list of n elements based on specific mathematical rules.\n    The i-th element is i! if i is even, or 1+...+i if i is odd (1-indexed).\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n    def _factorial(k: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the factorial of a non-negative integer k.\"\"\"\n        if k < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Factorial is not defined for negative numbers.\")\n        if k == 0:\n            return 1\n        result = 1\n        for j in range(1, k + 1):\n            result *= j\n        return result\n\n    def _sum_up_to(k: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the sum of integers from 1 to a positive integer k.\"\"\"\n        if k <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Sum up to k is only defined for positive k.\")\n        return k * (k + 1) // 2\n\n    result_list = []\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        if i % 2 == 0:  # i is even\n            value = _factorial(i)\n        else:  # i is odd\n            value = _sum_up_to(i)\n        result_list.append(value)\n    return result_list"}
