{"task_id": "HumanEval/131", "solution": "import math\ndef digits(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Given a positive integer n, return the product of the odd digits.\n    Return 0 if all digits are even.\n    For example:\n    digits(1)  == 1\n    digits(4)  == 0\n    digits(235) == 15\n    \"\"\"\n    all_digits = [int(d) for d in str(n)]\n    odd_digits = [d for d in all_digits if d % 2 != 0]\n\n    if not odd_digits:\n        return 0\n    \n    return math.prod(odd_digits)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/131", "solution": "def digits(n):\n    \"\"\"Given a positive integer n, return the product of its odd digits.\n    Return 0 if all digits are even.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 0: # Handle non-positive integers as per design and examples.\n        return 0\n\n    product = 1\n    found_odd_digit = False\n    temp_n = n\n\n    while temp_n > 0:\n        digit = temp_n % 10\n        if digit % 2 != 0: # Check if the digit is odd.\n            product *= digit\n            found_odd_digit = True\n        temp_n //= 10\n\n    return product if found_odd_digit else 0"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/130", "solution": "from typing import List\nclass InvalidInputError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for invalid input values.\"\"\"\n    pass\ndef tri(n: int) -> List[float]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Everyone knows Fibonacci sequence, it was studied deeply by mathematicians in\n    the last couple centuries. However, what people don't know is Tribonacci sequence.\n    Tribonacci sequence is defined by the recurrence:\n    tri(1) = 3\n    tri(n) = 1 + n / 2, if n is even.\n    tri(n) =  tri(n - 1) + tri(n - 2) + tri(n + 1), if n is odd.\n    For example:\n    tri(2) = 1 + (2 / 2) = 2\n    tri(4) = 3\n    tri(3) = tri(2) + tri(1) + tri(4)\n           = 2 + 3 + 3 = 8\n    You are given a non-negative integer number n, you have to a return a list of the\n    first n + 1 numbers of the Tribonacci sequence.\n    Examples:\n    tri(3) = [1, 3, 2, 8]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or n < 0:\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"Input 'n' must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n    cache = {}\n\n    def _calculate_term(k: int) -> float:\n        \"\"\"\n        Computes the k-th Tribonacci term using memoization and recurrence rules.\n        This helper function avoids redundant calculations.\n        \"\"\"\n        if k in cache:\n            return cache[k]\n\n        result: float\n        if k == 0:\n            result = 1.0\n        elif k == 1:\n            result = 3.0\n        elif k % 2 == 0:  # k is even and k > 1\n            result = 1.0 + k / 2.0\n        else:  # k is odd and k > 1\n            # For odd k, k+1 is always even, so tri(k+1) can be calculated directly.\n            term_k_minus_1 = _calculate_term(k - 1)\n            term_k_minus_2 = _calculate_term(k - 2)\n            term_k_plus_1 = 1.0 + (k + 1) / 2.0\n            result = term_k_minus_1 + term_k_minus_2 + term_k_plus_1\n        \n        cache[k] = result\n        return result\n\n    sequence = []\n    for i in range(n + 1):\n        sequence.append(_calculate_term(i))\n    \n    return sequence"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/131", "solution": "def digits(n):\n    \"\"\"Given a positive integer n, return the product of the odd digits.\n    Return 0 if all digits are even.\n    For example:\n    digits(1)  == 1\n    digits(4)  == 0\n    digits(235) == 15\n    \"\"\"\n    odd_digits = []\n    for digit_char in str(n):\n        digit = int(digit_char)\n        if digit % 2 != 0:\n            odd_digits.append(digit)\n\n    if not odd_digits:\n        return 0\n    \n    product = 1\n    for odd_digit in odd_digits:\n        product *= odd_digit\n    \n    return product"}
